.Dd August 27, 2026
.Dt LANDLOCK-SANDBOXER 1
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm landlock-sandboxer
.Nd run a command under a Landlock filesystem and TCP sandbox
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ev LL_FS_RO Ns = Ns Ar paths
.Ev LL_FS_RW Ns = Ns Ar paths
.Op Ev LL_TCP_BIND Ns = Ns Ar ports
.Op Ev LL_TCP_CONNECT Ns = Ns Ar ports
.Op Ev LL_SCOPED Ns = Ns Ar scopes
.Nm
.Ar command Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
builds a Landlock ruleset out of its environment, applies it to itself, and then
executes
.Ar command .
The ruleset is inherited by everything
.Ar command
goes on to execute and can never be widened again, so what is granted on the
command line is the most that any part of the resulting process tree can do.
.Pp
Landlock is unprivileged.
Nothing here needs root, a capability, a device or a namespace: a task asks the
kernel to restrict itself, and the kernel agrees.
That is what makes it different from every other sandbox on this system - a
user with no way to become root can still confine what they run.
.Pp
Two settings are mandatory, because a forgotten one would silently be a sandbox
that allows everything:
.Ev LL_FS_RO
and
.Ev LL_FS_RW .
Each is a colon-separated list of paths, and each path is granted along with
everything beneath it.
A path that does not exist is an error rather than an empty grant.
.Pp
The two port settings work the other way round, and the difference matters.
When one is not set at all the access it covers is left unhandled, and
unhandled means always allowed; set to the empty string it is handled with no
port in it, and nothing is permitted.
.Ev LL_SCOPED
has no such distinction - unset and empty both mean no scoping.
.Pp
The kernel's answer to the ruleset is all-or-nothing per operation and is not
logged by default.
A denied program sees
.Er EACCES ,
.Er EPERM
or
.Er ENOENT
from an ordinary system call and usually reports it as the file being missing,
so the first run of anything under
.Nm
is best done with
.Xr strace 1
beside it.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "LL_TCP_CONNECT"
.It Ev LL_FS_RO
Colon-separated paths that may be read, listed and executed.
Required.
.It Ev LL_FS_RW
Colon-separated paths that may additionally be written, created, removed,
renamed and truncated.
Required.
.It Ev LL_TCP_BIND
Colon-separated TCP ports the sandboxed process may bind.
Unset means every port; empty means none.
.It Ev LL_TCP_CONNECT
Colon-separated TCP ports the sandboxed process may connect to.
Unset means every port; empty means none.
.It Ev LL_SCOPED
Colon-separated letters naming what the sandbox may not reach outside itself:
.Ql a
for abstract Unix sockets, and
.Ql s
for signals.
.It Ev LL_FORCE_LOG
Set to
.Ql 1
to have the kernel audit denied requests.
A real sandboxer should not: it is one log record per denial, from a program
that is expected to be denied.
It is here to see that the policy is doing what it was asked to.
.El
.Pp
Each of these is removed from the environment before
.Ar command
runs, so a sandboxed program cannot read the policy it is under.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 1 without executing anything when the ruleset cannot be built - a missing
mandatory setting, a path that does not exist, or a kernel with no Landlock.
Otherwise it is replaced by
.Ar command
and the exit status is that command's.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Read-only everything the shell needs, one writable directory, no network at
all:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
LL_FS_RO="/usr:/etc:/lib64:/dev/urandom" \e
LL_FS_RW="/tmp/work:/dev/null" \e
LL_TCP_BIND="" LL_TCP_CONNECT="" \e
landlock-sandboxer /usr/bin/bash
.Ed
.Pp
A build that may write only its own tree:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
LL_FS_RO="/usr:/etc:/lib64" LL_FS_RW="${PWD}:/tmp" \e
landlock-sandboxer make
.Ed
.Pp
A client that may reach HTTPS and nothing else:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
LL_FS_RO="/usr:/etc:/lib64:/dev/urandom" \e
LL_FS_RW="/dev/null:/tmp" \e
LL_TCP_BIND="" LL_TCP_CONNECT="443" \e
landlock-sandboxer curl -sS https://example.com/
.Ed
.Pp
Check that the kernel has Landlock at all - this prints the usage and the ABI
version, or the hint that says the kernel was built without it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
landlock-sandboxer
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr capsh 1 ,
.Xr strace 1 ,
.Xr prctl 2
.Pp
.Pa /root/sowa-howto/sandboxing.txt
.Sh STANDARDS
Landlock is a Linux security module.
This kernel provides ABI version 7, which is what
.Nm
was compiled against, so every access type it knows about is one the kernel can
enforce.
.Sh AUTHORS
The program is
.Pa samples/landlock/sandboxer.c
